issues.evidence:
The EXP-0004 vs EXP-0005 contrast (prefill 13.4-13.8 s / ~6480 slow copies vs 1.1-3.0 s / 1-604) differs in all of the following at once:
- eviction procedure (none vs memory-pressure eviction, which trims other processes and raises headroom);
- RAM headroom (about 16.5-18.2 vs 19.7-21.1 GiB);
- code (the split upload path);
- what other processes were doing.

Within EXP-0005, r1 runs (lower headroom) had more prefill slow copies than r2 in every mmap arm except prof15000: off 604 vs 44, addr7842 465 vs 37, prof7842 322 vs 97, addr15000 335 vs 78, prof15000 144 vs 148. But r1 vs r2 is also ABBA position.

Disk reads from the first large copy to the end of prefill are still 1.2-3.9 GiB, so some re-reading or prefetch tail is present even with more headroom.

verdicts.corrected_text:
In this session, available RAM before each run was 19.7-21.1 GiB against the 20.82 GiB file, below the file in 7 of 12 runs. After eviction (0-7 of 256 probed expert pages cached), the mmap arms prefilled the 46-token prompt in 1.55-3.02 s with 37-604 slow copies of 6630-7728. The pageable off arm took 2.10 and 2.97 s with 44 and 604 slow; of its 6339 expert copies, 38 and 580 were slow, the rest of the count being 291 load-time uploads of non-expert weights. --load-mode none took 1.1 s with 1 slow copy, but it holds the experts in a 19275 MiB pinned buffer filled during a 15 s load, so it does not test the page cache. EXP-0004's pageable runs that followed other pageable runs took 13.4-13.8 s with 6423-6487 of 6630 slow, every expert copy in layers 0-39 included, at 16.5-17.3 GiB available.

Here the load did not read the whole file. The load burst read 19.05-19.78 GiB (r1) and 20.24-20.38 GiB (r2), about 0.5-0.8 GiB below available RAM. The experts that were slow at prefill are the unread tail by GGUF offset:
- layers 36-39 in off_r1 and addr7842_r1;
- layers 37-39 in addr15000_r1;
- layer 39 in every r2 off/addr run.

Hypothesis (level 3): a cold page cache by itself does not cause EXP-0004's 13.4 s regime. Prefill page faults scale with available RAM through how much of the file's tail the load leaves unread. The r1/r2 contrast is confounded with session order, but the per-run link from burst size to slow layers is direct. RAM shortfall alone does not explain EXP-0004, though. The same tail pattern would leave about the last 9-10 layers missing, yet all 40 were. And at the same RAM, prior state set the regime: pageable runs right after a pinned run (17.05-18.15 GiB) prefilled in 3.7-7.3 s (1018-2704 slow), against 13.4-13.6 s after pageable runs at 17.29-17.30 GiB. The 'load reads the whole file and evicts the start' mechanism does not fit this session. A pageable run after eviction at about 17 GiB available would separate RAM from prior state.
